ci(whitepaper): close PR #117 review findings — schema-level phrase ban, CI wiring, AST rewrite

PR #117 merged 2026-05-20 with an adversarial-review thread that the
operator-response comment promised to address in a follow-up. This is
that follow-up. The original merge happened while a plenipotentiary
Claude was waving things through; this PR repays the debt.

Findings closed:

  High 1 — Drift check not actually wired into CI.
    Added .github/workflows/whitepaper-metadata.yml: path-scoped trigger
    on whitepaper PDFs / registry / script / test changes, installs
    poppler-utils via apt, runs npm run test:whitepaper-metadata and
    the vitest suite under CI=true. Without this, the README's "CI
    fails on drift" sentence was aspirational.

  High 2 — pdfinfo missing returned 0 with warning, even in CI.
    Script now reads process.env.CI; absent pdfinfo with CI=true is a
    hard return-code-1 with an explicit error message. Local dev keeps
    the warn-and-skip behavior.

  High 3 — Forbidden-phrase grep violated NO KEYWORD-BASED NLP rule.
    Replaced the runtime substring scan with a TypeScript
    template-literal type. ForbiddenPhrase is a literal union; the
    NoForbidden<S> conditional type emits an explicit error string when
    a body field matches; defineWhitePapers<const T>(...) threads
    literal types through inference (DeepReadonly<WhitePaper>
    constraint preserves them) and intersects each paper with its
    validated form. A forbidden phrase anywhere in body prose collapses
    the offending paper's intersection to never at compile time. The
    `status` field stays plain string (explicitly exempt). The test
    file's role shrinks to "if tsc compiled, the constraint held."
    The hygiene block in whitePapers.test.ts now documents this as the
    canonical pattern for future keyword bans on data files.

  Medium 4 — sizeKb tolerance was 5% (±43 KB on 863 KB PDF).
    Tightened to max(2%, 4 KB): percentage catches drift on large PDFs
    (±17 KB on 863 KB), 4 KB floor keeps small LaTeX rebuilds from
    spurious failures. Updated size-tolerance tests accordingly.

  Medium 5 — rewriteMetadata regex was fragile (indent + first-match).
    Replaced the slice-finder + .replace() with a ts-morph AST rewrite.
    setInitializer() on the pages/sizeKb PropertyAssignment under each
    matching `id: '<known>'` object literal. ts-morph added to
    devDependencies. The new path also fixed the already-drifted anchor
    paper metadata (22→23 pages, 690→805 KB) — the AST rewrite proved
    itself in passing.

  Low 6 — pdfinfoAvailable() only caught ENOENT.
    Now catches any non-zero-status-99 failure (EACCES, ETIMEDOUT,
    EPIPE, corrupted binary), logs the specific code/message, and
    returns false. Lets the caller decide CI hard-fail vs local skip
    based on a real signal, not a partial one.

  Low 7 — README cited `pnpm --filter website-v2 test`.
    No pnpm-workspace.yaml exists. Replaced with
    `cd website-v2 && npm run fix:whitepaper-metadata` and added a
    pointer to the new GitHub Actions workflow.

Validation:
  cd website-v2
  npx tsc --noEmit            # clean
  npx vitest run src/data/whitePapers.test.ts   # 9/9 pass
  npm run test:whitepaper-metadata              # "in sync"

